<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rawanduz</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rawandiz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rawanduz is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Erbil</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> in Iraqi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a>, set on a rock above the deep gorge of the Rawanduz river near the Gali Ali Beg canyon. It was the capital of the Kurdish Soran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>, which under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-muhammad-kor">Mir Muhammad Kor</a> in the early 19th century made it a centre of arms production and military power, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> ended the emirate in 1836.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rawanduz.jpg" alt="Rawanduz" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Claus Weinberg · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rawanduz_-_Kurdistan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Rawandiz lies in the Soran district of the Erbil Governorate, close to the borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, 7 km from the centre of Soran city, 10 km east of the Bekhal Waterfall and 123 km from Erbil. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> mountains surround it, with Korek Mountain to the south, Hindren Mountain to the north, Zozik Mountain to the west and Bradasot Mountain to the east. The name means fortress of the Rawand and comes from the Kurdish noble family of the Rawwadi or Rawandi, a leading family of the Hadhbāni Kurdish tribe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the 10th to the 7th centuries BCE, in the time of the Assyrian kings, the area lay on the trading route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>. The town was founded by the Rawadid dynasty in the 10th century CE. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaldiran">Battle of Chaldiran</a> in 1514 it came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Bradost, and later under the Soran Emirate until 1836. A Yazidi manuscript of the kind called Mişûr, dedicated to the saint Pīr Khatīb Pisī and copied in 1862, names the town as Randūz among the places owing dues.</p><p>In 1915, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, Russians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> occupied the town, and in the Rawandiz massacre the Russian army and allied Assyrian militants killed much of its Kurdish Muslim population: after Nikolai Baratov's Cossacks recaptured it, only 20 per cent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> had survived. Turkish troops held it in 1922 until they were driven out at the end of the year, and the British army occupied it on 22 April 1923. Between 1928 and 1932 the British built a strategic road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Erbil</a> through Rawandiz to the Iranian border, directed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> engineer A. M. Hamilton.</p><p>Hamilton wrote in 1930 that the town had always been a place of grim deeds and bloody retributions, whose rulers had nearly all met violent deaths. The anthropologist Edmund Leach came in 1938 to study the Rawandiz Kurds; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> crisis cut his field trip short, but he published Social and Economic Organization of the Rowanduz Kurds two years later. In 1940 the town had 1,970 inhabitants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Rawandiz has a hot-summer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> climate, with very hot dry summers and cool wet winters. Its population is Kurdish. Hamilton reported that its gorge was said to be the finest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and a cable car 4 km long runs from the Bekhal station to Mount Korek, which has been developed as a resort.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rawandiz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He built a standing army, made muskets and cannon in <b>Rawanduz</b>, imposed order on the tribes and conquered neighbouring districts, including Bahdinan, while his campaigns against the Yazidis were notoriously brutal.</blockquote><a href="/state/soran-emirate">Soran emirate</a></li></ul></section>
